amdgpu-global-offset-hostcall: a fix to intel/llvm v7.2.0, applied by tools/intel_llvm_build.py.

A SYCL kernel that asks for a global id calls __spirv_BuiltInGlobalOffset, which the GlobalOffset pass
replaces only in codegen (with a parameter of the kernel's _with_offset clone, or 0).  The AMDGPU attributor
runs before that and took the call as an unknown callee, so every such kernel kept all the implicit inputs,
the hostcall buffer among them: 14,304 of XeStrata 0.2.2's 32,556 AMD kernels (0 of 48,084 with v7.1.1).
HIP refuses to launch a kernel with a hostcall buffer where the GPU has no PCIe atomics: a GPU passed through
to a virtual machine, or in a slot behind a chipset, failed at its first kernel with hipErrorIllegalState
(XeStrata's issues #40 and #54).  Fixed, the attributor takes the builtin as reading no implicit argument.
---
diff --git a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAttributor.cpp b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAttributor.cpp
index 630ffad96e..fe2d4076b1 100644
--- a/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAttributor.cpp
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAttributor.cpp
@@ -476,8 +476,19 @@ struct AAAMDAttributesFunction : public AAAMDAttributes {
         addKnownBits(Attr.first);
     }
 
-    if (F->isDeclaration())
+    if (F->isDeclaration()) {
+      // Modified 2026 by MistVVK and the XeStrata contributors: SYCL's global
+      // offset builtin reads no implicit argument.  The GlobalOffset pass
+      // replaces its calls in codegen, after this pass, with a parameter of the
+      // kernel's _with_offset clone or 0.  Taken as an unknown callee, it kept
+      // the hostcall buffer and every other implicit input on each kernel that
+      // asks for a global id, and HIP refuses to launch a kernel with a
+      // hostcall buffer where the GPU has no PCIe atomics (a GPU passed through
+      // to a virtual machine, or behind a chipset).
+      if (F->getName() == "_Z27__spirv_BuiltInGlobalOffseti")
+        addKnownBits(ALL_ARGUMENT_MASK);
       return;
+    }
 
     // Ignore functions with graphics calling conventions, these are currently
     // not allowed to have kernel arguments.
